1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,695
Mark Zuckerberg is back in the market with a new AI tool.

2
00:00:02,695 --> 00:00:11,935
[chuckles] And this new tool wants access to your emails, calendar, shopping accounts, and potentially even your money.

3
00:00:11,935 --> 00:00:15,615
Considering all of this is coming from Meta, it sounds a bit terrifying.

4
00:00:15,615 --> 00:00:18,575
So while introducing it, Mark did something very interesting.

5
00:00:18,575 --> 00:00:20,115
He talked about his daughters.

6
00:00:20,115 --> 00:00:26,595
Hi, I'm Asma. I research things I find fascinating, and today we'll discuss Muse, Meta's new personal AI agent.

7
00:00:26,595 --> 00:00:30,095
Unlike regular AI, Muse doesn't just tell you how to do something.

8
00:00:30,095 --> 00:00:31,695
It actually goes and does it.

9
00:00:31,695 --> 00:00:37,155
It can help you send emails, fill forms, book trips, and make purchases with approval, but it'll also keep

10
00:00:37,155 --> 00:00:40,915
on working after you close the app. It also remembers things about you.

11
00:00:40,915 --> 00:00:45,855
Very useful, but also very scary. If you think about it, it's a lot of access, which is

12
00:00:45,855 --> 00:00:49,335
why I find it very interesting the way he delivered the announcement.

13
00:00:49,335 --> 00:00:55,895
He talked about using Muse for baking projects with his three-year-old, finding mountain climbing permits for another daughter,

14
00:00:55,895 --> 00:00:58,475
and even turning a video game into a history lesson.

15
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:04,315
Now, I obviously can't prove that this was a PR strategy, but communication-wise, it does feel that way. AI

16
00:01:04,315 --> 00:01:10,175
with access to accounts and money sounds kind of scary, but AI helps dad bake with his daughter

17
00:01:10,175 --> 00:01:13,875
sounds pretty nice. And if you think about it, Meta really needs that trust.

18
00:01:13,875 --> 00:01:18,695
The launch of Muse was reportedly delayed because of security reasons as well, even though now the company

19
00:01:18,695 --> 00:01:21,895
says that it has built multiple safeguards into the product.

20
00:01:21,895 --> 00:01:27,555
But this whole thing really reminded me of WALL·E as well. The machines in WALL·E weren't really scary

21
00:01:27,555 --> 00:01:31,435
because they were evil. They were scary because they were super helpful.

22
00:01:31,435 --> 00:01:37,135
Humans became so comfortable with tech doing things for them that they stopped doing anything at all. I

23
00:01:37,135 --> 00:01:42,635
don't know about you, but I'm not particularly interested in becoming a fat person floating on a device and

24
00:01:42,635 --> 00:01:45,335
using their chubby fingers to put orders in a system.

25
00:01:45,595 --> 00:01:50,315
And yes, Muse is not exactly that kind of a product, but the tech is really moving towards

26
00:01:50,315 --> 00:01:54,675
that direction. So maybe the question is not if I can trust AI to do this for me,

27
00:01:54,675 --> 00:01:57,895
but how much of my life do I want AI to do on my behalf?

